1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. O-O Bc5 5. d4 exd4 6. e5 d5 7. exf6 dxc4 8. Re1+ Be6 9. Ng5 Qd5 10. Nc3 Qf5 11. Nce4 O-O-O 12. Nxe6 fxe6 13. g4 Qe5 14. Bg5 Rde8 15. fxg7 Rhg8 16. h4 d3 17. Nxc5 Qxc5 18. Bf6 Nb4 19. cxd3 Nxd3 20. Re3 Nf4 21. Qd4 Qxd4 22. Bxd4 Rd8 23. Be5 Nd3 24. Bf6 Rd6 25. h5 Kd7 26. Rf3 e5 27. h6 Ke6 28. Bg5 Rd7 29. Rf8 Rgxg7 30. hxg7 Rxg7 31. Be3 Rxg4+ 32. Kf1 Nxb2 33. Rb1 c3 34. Ke2 Ra4 35. Rg8 Rxa2 36. Rgg1 b5 37. Ra1 Rxa1 38. Rxa1 Na4 39. Kd3 Kd5 40. Bxa7 c5 41. Bb8 e4+ 42. Kc2 Kc4 43. Re1 Kd4 {"At this point time was called, and the position was adjudicated upon by Mr. Burn, who gave it as a draw." (The Field) "Played on Board 1 in the annual match between Oxford and Cambridge, which took place in the City of London Club on Monday, March 23. When time was called this game was unfinished and the position was very difficult, so much so that Mr. Amos Burn decided to adjudicate it at his leisure, and in doing so probably spent more time on analy­sis than had been spent on the actual game in pro­gress." (Oxford Chronicle and Reading Gazette, 3 April 1914) Sources: The Field, 28 March 1914; Oxford Chronicle and Reading Gazette, 3 April 1914; The People, 5 April 1914; Maidenhead Advertiser, 8 April 1914; Exeter and Plymouth Gazette, 14 April 1914} 1/2-1/2

